\section*{Question 4}

The graph of the function \(f\) shown above consists of two line segments. Let \(g\) be the function given by \(g(x)=\int_{0}^{x} f(t) d t\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find \(g(-1), g^{\prime}(-1)\), and \(g^{\prime \prime}(-1)\).
\item[(b)] For what values of \(x\) in the open interval \((-2,2)\) is \(g\) increasing? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(c)] For what values of \(x\) in the open interval \((-2,2)\) is the graph of \(g\) concave down? Explain your reasoning.
\item[(d)] On the axes provided, sketch the graph of \(g\) on the closed interval \([-2,2]\).
\end{itemize}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/4f5cfae5-963d-44a7-9935-271a1082e954-1.jpg?height=524&width=385&top_left_y=339&top_left_x=1633)
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] \(\quad g(-1)=\int_{0}^{-1} f(t) d t=-\int_{-1}^{0} f(t) d t=-\frac{3}{2}\) \(g^{\prime}(-1)=f(-1)=0\) \(g^{\prime \prime}(-1)=f^{\prime}(-1)=3\)
\end{itemize}
\[
3 \begin{cases}1: & g(-1) \\ 1: & g^{\prime}(-1) \\ 1: & g^{\prime \prime}(-1)\end{cases}
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(b)] \(g\) is increasing on \(-1<x<1\) because \(g^{\prime}(x)=f(x)>0\) on this interval.
\end{itemize}
\[
2 \begin{cases}1 & : \text { interval } \\ 1 & : \text { reason }\end{cases}
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)] The graph of \(g\) is concave down on \(0<x<2\) because \(g^{\prime \prime}(x)=f^{\prime}(x)<0\) on this interval. or because \(g^{\prime}(x)=f(x)\) is decreasing on this interval.
\end{itemize}
\[
2 \begin{cases}1: & \text { interval } \\ 1: & \text { reason }\end{cases}
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(d)]
